+// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// The debug HUD.
+//
+// Every number this port has been tuned by so far -- frame rate, damaged area,
+// blit cost, free memory -- has been read out of a log file pulled over the
+// network after the fact. That is fine for a measurement and useless while
+// somebody is holding the console: the moment you want to know *which* screen
+// is slow, or whether a scroll is the expensive thing, the answer has to be on
+// the panel next to the thing you are touching.
+//
+// So this draws the same window the FRAMES line reports into the corner of the
+// framebuffer, after the blit and directly into the display buffer. Flutter
+// knows nothing about it, which is deliberate: it costs the app nothing, needs
+// no widget, and works identically in every app in the corpus without a line of
+// Dart. It also means partial repaint cannot erase it -- the blit restores app
+// pixels underneath and the HUD is redrawn on top in the same call.
+//
+// It is drawn on the raster thread, in the present callback, and its own cost
+// is measured and reported separately so it can never be mistaken for the
+// blit's.
+
+// 5x7, one byte per column, bit 0 is the top row. The classic ASCII cell: it
+// is legible at 2x on a 960x544 panel held at arm's length, and small enough
+// that the whole table is smaller than one line of Skia.
+//
